WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.692
Film Courage: Something you said triggered
a question I have further down on my list

00:00:02.692 --> 00:00:07.029
but I’ll bump it up and that was you were
referring to the music video of the film which

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:12.270
you made while you were in law school and
then I watched a video where you say when

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:17.560
a book turns into a film, then a game and
then a comic book, that’s not transmedia,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.760
it’s multimedia.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.970
So can we discuss how many people think that
this is transmedia and dispel some of the

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:25.970
myths?

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:26.970
Houston Howard: Yeah.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.440
So especially in the 1980’s we had this
boom of the multimedia franchise.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.800
And multimedia is the same story that is adapted
across multiple mediums.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.500
You know, the book becomes the movie, that
movies become the graphic novel, that graphic

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.000
novel becomes the video game.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.489
It’s basically the same story and the same
plot over and over and over again, just expressed

00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:51.620
through multiple mediums.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:59.280
Transmedia is sort of this second cousin of
that and where instead of having the TWILIGHT

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.789
book become the TWILIGHT movie that becomes
the TWILIGHT video game that becomes the TWILIGHT

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:04.040
graphic novel.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:11.240
Now we have the books that then set up the
movie that then leads into the graphic novel

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.640
that then culminates in the video game all
the different touch points tell a completely

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:23.570
different story but in a way where all the
stories work together to tell one big story.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:34.659
That’s a different process but it was birthed
out of the necessity of the marketplace.

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:42.149
Having grown up in the 1980’s and the 1990’s,
the multimedia franchise was fine because

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:43.149
what else are you going to do?

00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:46.630
We didn’t have the 8,000 different entertainment
options that we have today.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:47.979
It was very limited.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:53.359
And so consumers and audiences were okay with
just experiencing the same story over and

00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:57.430
over and over again because there were very
limited amounts of options.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:05.890
Today, we now have 8,000 television channels,
we have cable, satellite, network, Netflix,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.670
Hulu, Amazon.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.170
We have millions of Youtube videos.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:16.060
We have access to every TV show ever made,
every movie ever made, every book ever written.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:21.310
We have millions of mobile games that are
available in our pocket anytime we want.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:27.170
And so now when we can entertain ourselves
with anything that we want, the marketplace

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.220
has been going for new stuff rather than repurpose
content.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:37.590
So if you give a consumer the option of a
video game where you already know the story

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:43.800
and a video game where you don’t know the
story, now they’re opting for the new story.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.190
They’re opting for the new book.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.660
They’re opting for the new movie because
they just don’t want to repurpose the same

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.470
thing over and over and over again.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:59.120
So when audiences demand new, new, new, you
have to figure out how to continually give

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.840
them a new story rather than repurpose story
which then has created this really interesting

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.000
opportunity in the marketplace but at the
same time, obstacle in the marketplace because

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.470
now we have to figure out how to continually
tell new stories because the multimedia franchise

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:18.500
just isn’t performing the way that it used
to perform.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:26.650
And so just like any good capitalistic system,
the market is the one that pushes toward change

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.140
and that’s what we’ve seen over the past
ten years.

